  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(12, 1fr);
  grid-gap: 10px;
  padding: 20px;
}

/* Style the article */
.article {
  background-color: #f7f7f7;
  padding: 20px;
  border: 1px solid #ddd;
}

/* Style the footer */
.footer {
  background-color: #333;
  color: #fff;
  padding: 10px;
  text-align: center;
}
```
This code defines a fluid grid, flexible images, and a media query for small screens. It also styles the header, navigation, main content, and footer using semantic HTML and CSS.

**COMMON MISTAKE:**

One common mistake that learners make when implementing responsive design and semantic HTML is not using the correct HTML elements to define the different parts of a web page. For example, using the `<div>` element to define a header or navigation section instead of the `<header>` or `<nav>` elements. This can make it difficult for search engines and assistive technologies to understand the content and structure of the web page.

**PRACTICE:**

1. Create a web page that displays a header, navigation, main content, and footer using semantic HTML and CSS. Make sure to use the correct HTML elements to define the different parts of the web page.
2. Add a media query to the web page to make it responsive on small screens. Use the `@media screen and (max-width: 768px)` query to apply styles only to screens with a maximum width of 768 pixels.
3. Use the BEM naming convention to define CSS classes for the web page. For example, use the `.header__nav--large` class to define a large navigation menu in the header section.
4. Create a web page that displays a grid of images using CSS grid and media queries. Make sure to use flexible images and a fluid grid to make the web page responsive on different screen sizes.

**Lesson 7: Implementing Responsive Design and Semantic HTML**

**OBJECTIVES:**

1. Explain the importance of responsive design in web development and how to implement it using CSS media queries.
2. Apply semantic HTML elements to improve the structure and accessibility of a web page.
3. Use CSS to create a visually appealing and responsive layout for a simple web page.

**TEACHING:**

In the previous lesson, we introduced the concept of user-centered design and the importance of accessibility in web development. In this lesson, we will dive deeper into the implementation of responsive design and semantic HTML. Responsive design is a crucial aspect of web development, as it ensures that a website is accessible and usable on various devices, including desktops, laptops, tablets, and mobile phones. To achieve this, we use CSS media queries to define different styles for different screen sizes.

The first principle of responsive design is to use fluid grids, which means that the layout of the website should adapt to the screen size. We can achieve this by using relative units, such as percentages or ems, instead of fixed units, such as pixels. For example, we can define the width of a container element as `width: 80%;` instead of `width: 800px;`. This way, the container will take up 80% of the screen width, regardless of the screen size.

Another important aspect of responsive design is the use of flexible images. We can achieve this by using the `max-width` property and setting it to 100%. This will ensure that the image does not exceed the width of its container and will scale down to fit smaller screens. We can also use the `height` property and set it to `auto` to maintain the aspect ratio of the image.

Semantic HTML is another crucial aspect of web development, as it provides a clear structure and meaning to the content of a web page. By using semantic HTML elements, such as `header`, `nav`, `article`, and `footer`, we can improve the accessibility and search engine optimization (SEO) of a website. For example, we can use the `header` element to define the header section of a website, which typically includes the logo, navigation menu, and other important links.

To implement responsive design and semantic HTML, we need to use CSS to define the styles and layout of a web page. We can use CSS media queries to define different styles for different screen sizes. For example, we can use the `@media` rule to define a style for screens with a maximum width of 768px, which is a common screen size for tablets and mobile phones. We can also use CSS to define the layout of a web page, including the width, height, margin, and padding of elements.

In addition to CSS media queries, we can also use CSS custom properties (variables) to define reusable values, such as colors and font sizes. This can help to simplify the maintenance and updating of a website, as we can define a value in one place and use it throughout the website.

**WORKED EXAMPLE:**

Let's create a simple web page that demonstrates responsive design and semantic HTML. We will create a header section with a logo and navigation menu, a main section with an article and an image, and a footer section with copyright information.

```html
<header>
  <nav>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="#">Home</a></li>
      <li><a href="#">About</a></li>
      <li><a href="#">Contact</a></li>
    </ul>
  </nav>
</header>
<main>
  <article>
    <h1>Responsive Design and Semantic HTML</h1>
    <p>This is a sample article that demonstrates responsive design and semantic HTML.</p>
    <img src="image.jpg" alt="Sample image">
  </article>
</main>
<footer>
  <p>&copy; 2023 Sample Website</p>
</footer>
```

We can then use CSS to define the styles and layout of the web page. We will use CSS media queries to define different styles for different screen sizes.

```css
/* Define the styles for large screens */
header {
  width: 100%;
  height: 100px;
  background-color: #333;
  color: #fff;
}

nav ul {
  list-style: none;
  margin: 0;
  padding: 0;
  display: flex;
  justify-content: space-between;
}

nav li {
  margin-right: 20px;
}

main {
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  align-items: center;
  padding: 20px;
}

article {
  width: 80%;
  margin: 0 auto;
}

img {
  max-width: 100%;
  height: auto;
  margin: 20px 0;
}

footer {
  width: 100%;
  height: 50px;
  background-color: #333;
  color: #fff;
  text-align: center;
  padding: 10px;
}

/* Define the styles for small screens */
@media (max-width: 768px) {
  header {
    height: 50px;
  }

  nav ul {
    flex-direction: column;
  }

  nav li {
    margin-right: 0;
    margin-bottom: 10px;
  }

  main {
    padding: 10px;
  }

  article {
    width: 100%;
  }

  img {
    margin: 10px 0;
  }

  footer {
    height: 30px;
    padding: 5px;
  }
}
```

**COMMON MISTAKE:**

One common mistake that learners make when implementing responsive design is to use fixed units, such as pixels, instead of relative units, such as percentages or ems. This can cause the layout of the website to break on smaller screens, as the elements will not adapt to the screen size. Another common mistake is to forget to define the `max-width` property for images, which can cause the images to exceed the width of their containers and distort the layout of the website.

**PRACTICE:**

1. Create a simple web page that demonstrates responsive design and semantic HTML. Use CSS media queries to define different styles for different screen sizes.
2. Use CSS custom properties (variables) to define reusable values, such as colors and font sizes. Update the styles of the web page to use these variables.
3. Add an image to the web page and use the `max-width` property to ensure that it does not exceed the width of its container.
4. Use the `@media` rule to define a style for screens with a maximum width of 480px, which is a common screen size for mobile phones. Update the layout of the web page to adapt to this screen size.

**Lesson 8: Implementing Responsive Design and Semantic HTML**

**OBJECTIVES:**

1. Explain the importance of responsive design in web development and how to implement it using CSS media queries.
2. Apply semantic HTML elements to improve the structure and accessibility of a web page.
3. Use CSS to create a visually appealing and responsive layout for a web page.

**TEACHING:**

In the previous lesson, we discussed the principles of user-centered design and accessibility. In this lesson, we will focus on implementing responsive design and semantic HTML to create a robust and accessible web page. Responsive design is crucial in today's web development as it allows web pages to adapt to different screen sizes and devices. This is achieved by using CSS media queries, which enable us to apply different styles based on specific conditions such as screen width or device type.

To implement responsive design, we need to use a combination of fluid grids, flexible images, and CSS media queries. A fluid grid is a grid system that uses relative units such as percentages or ems instead of fixed units like pixels. This allows the grid to adapt to different screen sizes and devices. Flexible images are images that can be scaled up or down without losing their quality. We can achieve this by using the `max-width` property and setting it to 100%. CSS media queries are used to apply different styles based on specific conditions. For example, we can use the `@media screen and (max-width: 768px)` query to apply styles only to screens with a maximum width of 768px.

Semantic HTML is another crucial aspect of web development. It involves using HTML elements that provide meaning to the structure of a web page. This improves the accessibility and readability of the web page, making it easier for search engines and assistive technologies to understand the content. Some common semantic HTML elements include `header`, `nav`, `article`, `section`, and `footer`. We should use these elements to define the different sections of our web page, making it easier to navigate and understand.

When implementing responsive design and semantic HTML, we need to consider the visual hierarchy of our web page. Visual hierarchy refers to the way we organize and prioritize content on our web page. We can achieve this by using Gestalt principles and typographic scale. Gestalt principles include proximity, similarity, continuity, and closure. Typographic scale involves using a consistent font size and line height to create a clear hierarchy of content. We can use the Modular Scale with a ratio of 1.25 or 1.333 to create a harmonious and balanced typographic scale.

In addition to responsive design and semantic HTML, we also need to consider performance optimization. Performance optimization involves minimizing the load time of our web page by optimizing images, compressing files, and using lazy loading. We can use tools like Google Lighthouse to audit our web page's performance and identify areas for improvement.

**WORKED EXAMPLE:**

Let's create a simple web page that demonstrates responsive design and semantic HTML. We will create a web page with a header, navigation bar, article section, and footer. We will use CSS media queries to make the web page responsive and semantic HTML elements to define the structure of the web page.

```html
