Finally, we add some JavaScript to demonstrate lazy loading:
```javascript
// script.js
const images = document.querySelectorAll('img');

images.forEach((image) => {
    image.addEventListener('load', () => {
        console.log('Image loaded!');
    });
});

// Lazy load images
const observer = new IntersectionObserver((entries) => {
    entries.forEach((entry) => {
        if (entry.isIntersecting) {
            const image = entry.target;
            image.src = image.dataset.src;
            observer.unobserve(image);
        }
    });
}, {
    rootMargin: '50px',
});

images.forEach((image) => {
    observer.observe(image);
});
```
This example demonstrates a basic responsive design that adapts to different screen sizes, using CSS media queries and semantic HTML. We also use lazy loading to defer the loading of images until they are actually needed.

**COMMON MISTAKE:**

One common mistake that learners make when implementing responsive design is to use fixed widths and heights instead of relative units, such as percentages or ems. This can cause the layout to break or become distorted on different screen sizes. To avoid this, it's essential to use relative units and to test the layout on different devices and screen sizes.

